00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.480
Evan is one of the winners of
the Visualize 2030 contest.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.060
He's a college student
at McMaster University

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:12.990
in Ontario, Canada.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.834
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.350
So tell the audience a
little bit about yourself.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:22.980
What are you busy
with these days?

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.855
EVAN SNARY: Yeah, so
right now, I'm currently

00:00:24.855 --> 00:00:26.230
attending McMaster University.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.530
But right now I'm on internship,
and at this internship,

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.840
I'm using Data Studio
and using visualizations

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.467
to present data to our
clients in a meaningful way.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.550
MINHAZ KAZI: How did you
become interested in data

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:37.925
visualization in
the first place?

00:00:37.925 --> 00:00:39.090
What got you into it?

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.548
EVAN SNARY: Yeah,
so I think it all

00:00:40.548 --> 00:00:42.400
started even before
my internship, where

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.395
on Reddit, I'd always
follow the subreddit

00:00:44.395 --> 00:00:46.090
called Data is
Beautiful, where people

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:51.400
post these visualizations to the
community for them to showcase.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.970
So I think that's really
where it all began

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.460
and my interest came from.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.252
MINHAZ KAZI: So you
were interested in data

00:00:57.252 --> 00:00:59.590
visualization even before
like you started building vis.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:02.740
But as you started building
your own visualizations,

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.550
how did you get
introduced to Data Studio?

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.047
And what has been your
experience with this tool?

00:01:07.047 --> 00:01:09.130
EVAN SNARY: Yeah, so to
answer the first question,

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.280
I think at my
current internship,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.840
we use Data Studio to
present data to our clients

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.045
and provide insights using
this visualization software.

00:01:17.045 --> 00:01:20.050
And I think stepping into the
software, that my experience

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.390
has been great overall
and its ease of use

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:23.950
really stepped out to me.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.340
It just so easy to use.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.170
And when I first started
on my internship,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.795
I had no experience
with it, but yet, I

00:01:28.795 --> 00:01:31.540
could still build these
dashboards for our clientele.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.593
That just shows how easy
it really is to use.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.260
MINHAZ KAZI: You use
Data Studio and you

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.020
build a vis for the
Visualize 2030 contest,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.710
and that was actually one of
the winning entries there.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:42.910
So congrats on that.

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:44.140
EVAN SNARY: Thank you.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.640
MINHAZ KAZI: Maybe you can take
the audience through the vis

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.370
and tell them the
story you are trying

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.370
to tell throughout
the visualization.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.870
EVAN SNARY: For sure, yeah, if
you want to take a look here.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.240
Yeah, so for my
Visualize 2030 project,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:59.290
I wanted to focus in on two
of the SDGs of no poverty

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.650
and climate action.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.450
So that's what I really focused
on with my interactive vis.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.550
So as you scroll
down, I kind of showed

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.919
statistics on what's
happening in the world today

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:12.230
and just bring awareness in
general to what's happening.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.990
And one of the key
findings I found out

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:16.900
that 71% of the total
worldwide emissions

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.510
come from these high
income countries.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.500
And we're actually noticing
an increase with middle income

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.550
countries, as you can see here.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:28.480
As they move from low income to
middle income classification,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.210
we're noticing huge
CO2 emission increases

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.070
per capita for these nations.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:37.270
So as you can see, I continued
to add interactive vises

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:40.210
to my Data Studio
project, as I really

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.550
wanted to give the audience
a way to interact with it

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.400
and find their own
conclusions to the problems.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.000
And here, people are able to
select an individual country

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.475
and really dive deeper into,
say, a country of interest,

00:02:51.475 --> 00:02:54.130
or if they want to see
just in particular what's

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.300
happening in a certain
region of the world,

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.030
they're able to see
specifically what's

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.580
happening with the
poverty improvement, GDP

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.180
growth, and climate
action in these nations.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.490
So I also included
what must be done

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.740
and what can we do to
make the problem better.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:10.390
And I think the key
takeaway for me,

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.430
was that we're going
to need to find

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:16.530
a way for these low countries
to develop sustainably,

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.280
whether that's through
high income country

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.415
aid or worldwide
international funding.

00:03:21.415 --> 00:03:23.665
We're going to need to find
a way for these low income

00:03:23.665 --> 00:03:25.360
countries to
develop sustainably,

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.240
so we don't decrease
poverty, but also

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.380
increase climate action
at the same time.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.380
MINHAZ KAZI: What do you
think about Data Studio

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.100
as a tool for anyone that's
trying to do data storytelling

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.732
and trying to bring
social change?

00:03:38.732 --> 00:03:40.690
EVAN SNARY: Yeah, so I
think really we all know

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:42.340
that words only go so far.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.360
And I think providing insights
through graphs or visuals

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.880
sometimes provides a better
context on these social change

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:49.850
issues.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.690
So I think Data Studio really
provides an easy and accessible

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.750
way for people to use
another platform to reach out

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.320
and try to provide change
to these problems we

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.620
face in the world today.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:00.995
MINHAZ KAZI: So
for someone who's

00:04:00.995 --> 00:04:02.770
just getting started
with the tool, who's

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.800
someone that doesn't have
any experience in building

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.350
their own data
visualizations or doesn't

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:11.050
have that much experience
in data storytelling,

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.220
how can they get started?

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:15.790
And what tips would
you give them to have

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:17.170
a really compelling story?

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:17.410
EVAN SNARY: Right.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:18.610
So I think that's
something actually

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.402
that my co-workers
always talk about a lot.

00:04:20.402 --> 00:04:22.990
And I think the very first
thing is just start simple.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:26.270
Whether it's one
graph or one bar chart

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.612
or one visualization
for that matter,

00:04:28.612 --> 00:04:30.320
it really doesn't
matter where you start.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.760
And I think building off that
one visualization is important.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.830
And don't try to do
too much at once.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.620
And really just start simple,
and start with just anything,

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:39.170
really.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.570
MINHAZ KAZI: Evan, thanks
again for being on the show.

